      "math_academy_id": "q-42492",
      "sequence_position": 6,
      "problem": "Suppose $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $det(A)=8.$ The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operations ${R}_{1}:=2{R}_{1}$ and ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2}.$ What is the value of $det(B)?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2}$ involves adding a multiple of one row to another row,  so it does not change the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{1}:=2{R}_{1}$ involves multiplying a row by $2,$ so the determinant is multiplied by $2$ as well.\n\nTherefore, we only have to multiply the determinant by $2.$ Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =2det(A) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
